.TH EXPAND 1.SH NAMEexpand \- turn tabs into spaces.SH SYNOPSIS\fBexpand\fR[\-t tab1[,tab2...]] [\-i] [file ...].SH DESCRIPTION\fBexpand\fRis GNU expand, from the textutils.  It copies each named file, or thestandard input, to the standard output with every tab replaced by thespaces that reach the next tab stop.  Stops are eight columns apartunless\fB\-t\fRsays otherwise..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-t\fI n\fRstops every\fIn\fRcolumns.\fB\-4\fRmeans the same as\fB\-t 4\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-t\fI n1,n2,...\fRstops at exactly those columns, counting from zero; tabs beyond the lastone become single spaces.  Long form\fB+tabs=\fIlist\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-i\fRexpand only the tabs at the start of a line, before any othercharacter.  Long form\fB+initial\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfexpand prog.c > prog.txt      tabs at every eighth columnexpand -t 4 prog.c            tabs at every fourth.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRYour own OS-9 may have a utility of this name; see\fBDOC/README-NAMES\fRfor which one a bare\fBexpand\fRruns..SH SEE ALSOunexpand, detab